+# Beneficiary program enrollment and assigned schedule
+
+Phase 3 adds `GET /api/v1/mobile/beneficiaries/me/overview` after the authentication and document patches. It uses the existing beneficiary mobile session and tables; no migration or dependencies are added.
+
+The response is `{ success: true, data: { enrollments, nextSchedule } }`, with `Cache-Control: private, no-store`. The authenticated account determines ownership; supplied beneficiary IDs do not change the account being read. Staff JWTs cannot authenticate to this route.
+
+Each enrollment contains `id`, `enrollmentDate` (YYYY-MM-DD), lowercase `status`, `reviewNotes`, nullable ISO `reviewedAt`, and `program`. Program fields are `id`, `code`, `name`, `type`, `description`, and numeric or null `grantAmount`. A missing grant stays null. All saved enrollment statuses are returned, including correction requests, rejection, suspension, and withdrawal. Staff IDs, other beneficiaries, eligibility snapshots, program budgets, and internal criteria are excluded.
+
+`nextSchedule` is null until staff publish an eligible assignment. Otherwise it contains `id`, `distributionId`, `title`, `date`, `slotStart`, `slotEnd`, `queueNumber`, `status`, `location`, and `program`. It selects the earliest unexpired SCHEDULED/CHECKED_IN assignment in an OPEN distribution with an ACTIVE program and an ALLOCATED allocation linked to an APPROVED enrollment. Closed slots are hidden; FULL slots remain visible to their assigned beneficiary. Dates and times come from the assigned session in Asia/Manila, and the venue comes from that slot, including sessions on another day or at another venue. Cancelled/missed schedules, cancelled/claimed allocations, inactive programs, and draft/closed/cancelled distributions are hidden.
+
+The endpoint is read-only. It does not approve enrollment, assign a program, generate or change slots, publish an event, verify identity, issue QR credentials, release a claim, or credit a wallet. Existing staff permissions and independent enrollment review remain in effect. Mobile currently views its own assigned programs; a general program catalog is separate work.
+
+## Verify
+
+From the mobile repository, run `npm.cmd run test:shared` with `DATABASE_URL` set to a separate migrated test PostgreSQL instance and the local shared backend configured for development mobile authentication. The test exercises the actual mobile TypeScript client and HTTP API: staff submission, review/correction/resubmission/approval, draft scheduling, rescheduling, publication, earliest selection, account isolation, hidden ineligible records, local session dates/venues, nullable amounts, private DTOs, and logout. It also checks the preceding account/profile/document workflow and removes its synthetic fixtures. Keep embedded Prisma tests separate from the browser instance.
+
+## Manual browser check
+
+1. Sign in on mobile and refresh Home and Profile > Enrollment status. An unassigned account shows empty states.
+2. An authorized administrator/facilitator submits that beneficiary to an active program using the staff web. A different authorized reviewer starts review and requests corrections or approves it. Refresh mobile status to see the decision, exact feedback, and review time.
+3. Authorized staff prepare a draft distribution, its sessions and allocations, then assign or reschedule the approved beneficiary while the event is still draft. The mobile schedule stays empty during preparation.
+4. Staff open the distribution after its readiness checks pass. Tap Refresh schedule on mobile; check the session's date, time, venue and queue number against the staff record.
+
+QR issuance, wallet/claims, shared notifications, real SMS and biometric matching are later mobile integration work. API/build checks and controlled browser screenshots do not replace a physical Android walkthrough or this complete manual staff scheduling test.
